# 32. Functions and declarations

A **function** is a name, a signature and a body: an expression in HQL that
gives the result. A **declaration** is a name and a signature with no body:
the result comes from a function written in Rust. A program calls both the
same way, and the checker types a call from the signature alone, so nothing
at the call says which of the two it reaches.

This chapter is about the second kind. Every function of the standard
library that a program cannot write for itself, from `abs` to `split`, is a
declaration, and [[learn#part-v-extending-hql|Part V]] is about adding
declarations of your own.

## A function has a body

[[04-functions|Chapter 4]] declares functions. The body follows `=`, and its
type is checked against the result the signature states:

```hql
fn double(n: Int) : Int = n * 2

double(21)
```

```text
42
```

Everything `double` does is in the program. Removing the Rust that
implements `hql` would leave the function exactly as it is written.

## A declaration has none

The same signature without `=` is a declaration. It states what a call takes
and gives, and nothing about how:

```hql
abs(-3)
```

```text
3
```

`abs` is declared in the standard module `math` as
`fn abs(self: Int) : Int`. The line has no body, because no HQL expression
defines the absolute value of an `Int` without already having one. The
result comes from a Rust function inside the `hql` binary.

A declaration is one half of a pair. The other half is the Rust function,
and the pair is joined by an **extension**: the unit that implements the
declarations of a package's modules. A declaration in a file that no
extension implements is checked and binds no name:

```hql
fn area(side: Float) : Float

area(2.0f)
```

```text
check error[unknown-name]: `area` is not bound
 --> <expression>:3:1
  |
3 | area(2.0f)
  | ^^^^^^^^^^
```

The declaration itself is accepted: its types exist and its name is free.
The call is refused because a program has no extension, so nothing
implements `area`.

## Which one to write

| | A function | A declaration |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Written in | HQL | HQL for the signature, Rust for the result |
| Lives in | a program or a module of a workspace | a module of a package |
| Needs | nothing beyond `hql` | a Rust toolchain to build the package |
| Reaches | what HQL expresses | anything a Rust crate does |
| Changes take effect | on the next run | after the package is rebuilt |

A function is the default. A declaration is for what HQL cannot express or
should not repeat: a numeric routine a Rust crate already provides, a file
format, a protocol, a computation whose speed matters.

